    "body": "OK this is ready to merge. I was initially worried there was more to figure with the interaction between shared workers, web locks, and bf-cache, but I don't think there's any work to do in this PR.\r\n\r\nThere are some interesting forced-eviction scenarios to think about:\r\n\r\n 1. Doc1 opens Doc2 and a dedicated worker. If Doc1 goes into the bf-cache, its worker is suspendable. If Doc2 requests a web lock held by either Doc1 or its dedicated worker (both frozen), some browsers <!-- Chromium --> evict Doc1 from the bf-cache so that Doc2 doesn't have to wait forever on the lock.\r\n 2. Similarly, if a shared worker holds a lock but all of its client documents are in the bf-cache, the shared worker is suspendable. If another document in the same storage partition as the lock (but not a client of the shared worker) requests the lock, some browsers <!-- Chromium --> evict all of the shared worker's documents, kill the worker, and release the lock, so the requesting document doesn't wait forever on it.\r\n    1. In contrast, if that shared worker has at least one fully active client, it is not suspendable. When that other non-client, same-storage-partition doc comes around and requests the lock, we can't just kill the worker and release the lock. The requestor has to wait.\r\n 4. Doc1 and Doc2 connect to the same shared worker. Doc1 holds a lock and goes into the bf-cache. If either the shared worker or Doc2 request the lock, some browsers <!-- Chromium --> evict Doc1 from the bf-cache.\r\n\r\nWe don't spec when a browser must evict documents from the bf-cache, I think. But we probably want to offer more guidance or otherwise draw attention to these scenarios in which browsers may want to perform eviction. Maybe that can be part of https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/11520.\r\n\r\nAnyways, the reason any of this relates to extended lifetime shared workers at all, is because (2) above. Extended lifetime shared workers basically turn (2) into (2.i), by letting a shared worker with no active clients _keep running_ as if it did have an active client. This allows for a footgun captured by @fergald in https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/10997#issuecomment-3105509844, where a shared worker is designed to hold a lock until a client document tells it to release, but that client first goes into the bf-cache. A second document that comes around and requests the lock will be waiting for N minutes (the duration of the extended lifetime) on the lock. Such a case is caused by a poorly designed worker and doesn't need special treatment in the spec, besides optional guidance as part of the work above.",
